#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079538} :activity/id #uuid "99731adb-b075-5d4b-865c-6112280a68cf" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049131 :topic/id #uuid "dd6da6f3-04b6-5a5c-ad43-d66c3a2a88c4" :topic/math-academy-id 649} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079538 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079684 :tutorial/content "In many real situations, a quantity decreases quickly at first, but then the decrease slows down and the quantity levels off near some fixed value.\n\nFor example, consider a cup of hot coffee placed in a room. At first, the coffee cools rapidly because it is much hotter than the surrounding air. As time passes and the coffee temperature gets closer to room temperature, the cooling slows down. Eventually, the temperature levels off and approaches the room temperature rather than continuing to decrease.\n\nThis type of behavior is described by an **inhibited decay model**.\n\nAn *inhibited decay equation* models situations where the rate at which a quantity $P$ decreases depends on how far it is from a target value $a$, called the **equilibrium value**. This equilibrium value represents the level that the quantity approaches over time.\n\nAn inhibited decay equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -r(P-a),\n$$\n\nand an inhibited decay initial value problem has the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}P}{\\mathrm{d}t} = -r(P-a), \\qquad P(0) = P_0\n$$\n\nwhere $r > 0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $P > a$ for all $t > 0.$\n\nIn this model, the equilibrium value $a$ acts as a lower bound. The solution decreases over time but never drops below $a,$ instead approaching it more and more slowly. The figure below illustrates this behavior.\n\n![](images/1b/1b68f547db4090382b3fee626d029c619448b1c9adafe8b50312d162ff85e623.png)\n\n**Note:** Inhibited growth and inhibited decay use the same basic idea: the rate of change is proportional to the difference between $P$ and the equilibrium value $a$. The only difference is whether the solution approaches $a$ from below (growth) or from above (decay).\n\nInhibited decay models can be written in expanded form. To identify an expanded equation as inhibited decay, we factorise out the *negative* coefficient of $P.$ For example, the equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = 20-5P,\n$$\n\n*is* an inhibited decay equation for $P > 4$ and $t > 0,$ since it can be expressed with $r=5$ and $a=4$ as\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}P}{\\textrm{d}t} = -5(P-4).\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "da7cd802-d46b-546f-bdc3-8a783a8bb999" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5513 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "8b8000c0-e0de-53fb-a0bd-c942ac0f5e81" :step/math-academy-id 584874 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079539}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079539 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078582 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078374 :question/id #uuid "9d7fbff5-7db5-53e4-a17e-3fb7ac7a0069" :question/math-academy-id "e-8957"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0709c667-bc10-5bd5-a383-3fd6ca332361" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048127}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078704} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078705} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078706} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243913} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243914} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186376014} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445956} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445957} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445959}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Inhibited Decay Equations and Initial Value Problems"} :step/id #uuid "21c46d35-42c5-51bc-8679-d81c11066483" :step/math-academy-id 584875 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079540}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079540 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079671 :tutorial/content "Now that we’ve practiced identifying inhibited decay models, let’s look at a classic example: **Newton’s law of cooling**.\n\nThis law describes how the temperature of an object changes when it is placed in an environment with a different temperature. Formally, it states that\n\n*The rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings.*\n\nThis leads to the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-T_S),\n$$\n\nwhere $T_S$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nLet’s put this into action. Suppose a thermometer is placed in a pot of boiling water and reads $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ The pot is then placed in a $20^\\circ\\textrm{C}$ room, and the thermometer's reading begins to decrease.\n\nGiven that after $7$ minutes its reading is $60^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ let's model the thermometer's temperature after $t$ minutes.\n\nIn this case, the temperature of the surroundings is $T_S = 20^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, by Newton's law of cooling, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}T}{\\textrm{d}t} = -k(T-20),\n$$\n\nwhere $T(t)$ is the temperature reading of the thermometer and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{\\text{d}T}{T-20} & =-k\\int \\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-20| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-20 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-20 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-20 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =20+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K=e^C$ is a constant of integration.\n\nWe'll determine the value of $K$ and $k$ in the next slide." :tutorial/id #uuid "f58653bc-cafb-532a-b92d-8183054c181b" :tutorial/math-academy-id 4075 :tutorial/title "Newton's Law of Cooling"} :step/id #uuid "2253199d-d2ca-53d8-a2f6-aa2bd0cd82fd" :step/math-academy-id 584880 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079555}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079541 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078583 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078340 :question/id #uuid "8012ecda-d2f4-52de-99de-7b2ebd635661" :question/math-academy-id "e-6676"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "74a902da-f299-54a3-8e93-ad2716d0e887" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078707} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078879} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243972} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243980} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243982} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186243983} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399580} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186445958}] :knowledge-point/title "Modeling Inhibited Decay"} :step/id #uuid "7f3084d6-abb3-5bf6-aedb-e65df0e1523b" :step/math-academy-id 584881 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079542}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079542 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078584 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078341 :question/id #uuid "e3e001a8-9294-59a4-82e6-b1122afdc72f" :question/math-academy-id "e-6677"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bfe4679b-9724-5502-89d4-05e7661a1b5f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050905}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078743} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078744} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244024} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244026} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244027} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244028} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186395762} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487327}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inhibited Decay Model"} :step/id #uuid "ae0595c0-ae02-56d3-a449-bb3345ccbe25" :step/math-academy-id 584884} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079555 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079619 :tutorial/content "From the previous slide, the temperature of the thermometer is given by\n\n$$\nT = 20 + Ke^{-kt},\n$$\n\nwhere $K$ and $k$ are constants to be determined.\n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the water was $100^\\circ\\textrm{C}.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0) = 100{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}100 & =20+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 100 & =20+K \\\\ K & =80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have the following expression for $T(t){:}$\n\n$$\nT(t) = 20 + 80e^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe can compute $k$ using the fact that $T(7)=60{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =20+80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ 40 & =80{e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2} & ={e}^{-7k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2}) & =-7k \\\\ -\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =-7k \\\\ k & =\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the temperature, in $^\\circ\\textrm{C},$ of the thermometer after $t$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT = 20 + 80e^{-t\\ln(2)/7}.\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "7811364e-9b00-5478-8f01-6f3ddfee6f58" :tutorial/math-academy-id 14906 :tutorial/title "Newton's Law of Cooling (Continued)"} :step/id #uuid "7feac704-66a6-58a5-be02-6d1d595f029b" :step/math-academy-id 608082 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186079541}}] :activity/title "Inhibited Decay Models With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186078671}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046659} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487426} :activity/id #uuid "4ae75f36-a304-5922-b635-26c88b77267f" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049131 :topic/id #uuid "dd6da6f3-04b6-5a5c-ad43-d66c3a2a88c4" :topic/math-academy-id 649} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487414 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399580 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5f5f9e59-3c36-5807-b8f1-60d5c867aee4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=kT(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399582} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ef51c8-be4a-5a5f-b4b6-7b12be0bb5cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-kT(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399583} {:answer/id #uuid "3f26a705-024d-5901-899b-988f84681bf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T-21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399584} {:answer/id #uuid "49678131-628e-553a-9844-5179f76e8b91" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=k(T+21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399585} {:answer/id #uuid "e7d4bea4-992f-5fb1-be9b-5bc63898ddea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{T}^{′}(t)=-k(T+21)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399586}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186399584} :answer-field/id #uuid "8c7c9128-4613-5828-914f-598e6ef01189" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186399581}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d44d9a6b-035e-5168-a3f6-72ab2e21a7d8" :question/math-academy-id "q-106878" :question/problem "The rate at which a body cools is proportional to the difference between its temperature and the temperature of its surroundings. Let $T(t)$ be the temperature of the body in degrees Celsius, and let $t>0$ be the time in minutes. If the temperature of the surroundings is ${21}^{∘}\\text{C},$ which differential equation could be used to model the temperature of the liquid? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={21}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-21).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "2aa81c5c-5cf6-5623-b70e-2dcb0c85aaa1" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487433}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487419 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487327 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "339a07cb-65ed-5553-99c1-8ac87a3d2893" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{98}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486705} {:answer/id #uuid "43b562cd-2db1-5728-88b6-e6241152ba5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{126}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486741} {:answer/id #uuid "9d515764-afcd-5111-817f-5442e35ae0ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{112}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486947} {:answer/id #uuid "b82eed89-d5fa-5f72-8196-eb6c23acabba" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{92}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486997} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9f3273-c38d-5046-8ba2-d45f97de8bb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{134}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487106}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186486741} :answer-field/id #uuid "3746981f-0e79-5e47-8df3-c9e044188fa9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186487160}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "533feaf7-7a54-5e3c-8829-f3324ed43a28" :question/math-academy-id "q-107128" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Fahrenheit, of an exhaust pipe of a motorcycle can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.08(T-74)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the exhaust pipe is ${140}^{∘}F,$ calculate its temperature after $3$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the exhaust pipe of a motorcycle is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.08(T-74).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-74} & =-0.08\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-74} & =-\\int 0.08\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-74| & =-0.08t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-74>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-74 & ={e}^{-0.08t+C} \\\\ T-74 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.08t} \\\\ T-74 & =K{e}^{-0.08t} \\\\ T & =74+K{e}^{-0.08t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the exhaust pipe was ${140}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=140,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}140 & =74+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 140 & =74+K \\\\ K & =66\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=74+66{e}^{-0.08t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the exhaust pipe after $t=3$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(3)=74+66{e}^{-0.08\\cdot 3}≈{126}^{∘}\\text{F},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} :step/id #uuid "36644ad5-26bc-5680-9a8d-2d22a7eb2188"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487426 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078879 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "342581f4-cabc-5059-a251-2692ca6f27ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-64(T-100)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186321542} {:answer/id #uuid "71be98e8-1e44-50bb-9bb9-b32dd2edc859" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-100(T-64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322040} {:answer/id #uuid "d2e6a8e3-742a-59bc-850f-65fe5a3e9e9c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-100)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322796} {:answer/id #uuid "e62e99ed-fb8f-592b-af00-7b9afa2790e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-164)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186322949} {:answer/id #uuid "ec9ef3f2-b765-5498-b81e-dd99e9dc25d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323010}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186323010} :answer-field/id #uuid "53b01d91-18e1-599b-8abf-64d7291ac4f2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186323328}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "58a15628-6a07-582f-a2d4-e1c7a7c6e77c" :question/math-academy-id "q-37365" :question/problem "A medical doctor records the temperature of ${100}^{∘}\\text{F}$ using a thermometer. After the measurement is taken, the temperature reading $T(t)$ of the thermometer decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling. Here, $T$ is measured in degrees Fahrenheit, and $t>0$ is the time in minutes. If the room temperature is ${64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ which differential equation could be used to model the thermometer reading? Assume that $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality." :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={64}^{∘}\\text{F},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-64).\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "5931805b-d9b9-5298-a1d9-33e2c2616d14" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487414}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487433 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186244026 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04eda6ac-1a0c-52d3-accd-9e4287c8ed9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{198}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241219} {:answer/id #uuid "1769c61b-0297-5fb6-9734-e3f5580daf02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{223}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186241380} {:answer/id #uuid "65c47c3d-f35e-5489-ade5-8d1e2e7968d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{194}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242096} {:answer/id #uuid "728037c7-4e08-5de7-8ff2-e11877f70cbf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{201}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186242193} {:answer/id #uuid "dcfe89bb-415b-5c7b-84d6-7bacf9027ad1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{189}^{∘}\\text{F}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243147}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186242193} :answer-field/id #uuid "393a690d-1e4c-5074-9abc-2b65ddbc8354" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186243547}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f919d480-6159-52e5-bf01-76d804f5fa17" :question/math-academy-id "q-107120" :question/problem "The temperature $T(t),$ in degrees Fahrenheit, of a fresh-baked cake can be modeled by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70)\n$$\n\nwhere $t>0$ is the time, measured in minutes. If the initial temperature of the cake is ${340}^{∘}F,$ calculate the temperature of the cake after $8$ minutes. Round your answer to the nearest integer." :question/worked-solution "We're told that the temperature of the fresh-baked cake is modeled by the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dT}{dt}=-0.09(T-70).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-0.09\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-70} & =-\\int 0.09\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-70| & =-0.09t+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-70>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-70 & ={e}^{-0.09t+C} \\\\ T-70 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T-70 & =K{e}^{-0.09t} \\\\ T & =70+K{e}^{-0.09t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature of the cake was ${340}^{∘}F.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=340,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}340 & =70+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 340 & =70+K \\\\ K & =270\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to our initial value problem is\n\n$$\nT(t)=70+270{e}^{-0.09t}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the temperature of the cake after $t=8$ minutes is\n\n$$\nT(8)=70+270{e}^{-0.09\\cdot 8}≈{201}^{∘}\\text{F},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest integer."} :step/id #uuid "7ae547ec-c32b-530d-a28e-36ed80d0553b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487435}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487435 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397884 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "12647afe-fb4f-5f2b-9326-19bf07c40a1b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "23\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397886} {:answer/id #uuid "ebd2a414-b8e6-52ba-9a39-7d6bbb93cce4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "31\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397887} {:answer/id #uuid "6612145e-ab64-58c5-8ac9-574cb5ec22a1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "45\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397888} {:answer/id #uuid "6ed38fbd-9ab4-5313-a9e7-428d7d2e672f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "34\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397889} {:answer/id #uuid "6e2e45ac-2ae6-5e93-b31c-67cedb5a0fae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28\\,\\text{min}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397890}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186397889} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad59f961-3401-5e50-8b21-92d3facaf654" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186397885}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fee906ee-def7-5194-a87e-a1f77108aa56" :question/math-academy-id "q-107125" :question/problem "Simon measures the temperature of the tea in his cup, and the thermometer displays a reading of ${60}^{∘}C.$ After $10$ minutes, Simon repeats the measurement and the thermometer displays a reading of  ${52}^{∘}C.$ To the nearest minute, how long does it take for the tea to decrease its temperature to ${40}^{∘}C$ if the temperature in the room is ${28}^{∘}C?$ \n\n*Hint: You may assume that the temperature $T(t)$ of the tea in Simon's cup decreases at a rate that obeys Newton's law of cooling, where $T$ is measured in degrees Celsius and $t>0$ is the time, in minutes, since the first measurement.*" :question/worked-solution "Newton's law of cooling states that the rate at which the temperature $T(t)$ of a body changes is proportional to the difference between its temperature and its surroundings. Therefore, we have the inhibited decay equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-{T}_{S}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${T}_{S}$ is the temperature of the surroundings, and $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nIn our case, ${T}_{S}={28}^{∘}\\text{C},$ and we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}T}{\\text{d}t}=-k(T-28).\n$$\n\nFirst, we integrate our differential equation by separating the variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\,dt \\\\ \\int \\frac{dT}{T-28} & =-k\\int dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡|T-28| & =-kt+C\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this is the solution to an inhibited decay equation, we have $T-28>0$ for all $t>0.$\nNext, we solve for $T(t),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T-28 & ={e}^{-kt+C} \\\\ T-28 & ={e}^{C}{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T-28 & =K{e}^{-kt} \\\\ T & =28+K{e}^{-kt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that $K={e}^{C}$ is a constant of integration. \n\nWe're told that the initial temperature reading was ${60}^{∘}C.$ So, we apply the initial condition $T(0)=60,$ and solve for $K:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}60 & =28+K{e}^{0} \\\\ 60 & =28+K \\\\ K & =32\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following expression for $T(t):$\n\n$$\nT(t)=28+32{e}^{-kt}\n$$\n\nWe're also told that, after $10$ minutes, the thermometer reading is ${52}^{∘}C.$ So, applying the condition $T(10)=52,$ we can solve for $k:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}52 & =28+32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ 24 & =32{e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4} & ={e}^{-10k} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4}) & =-10k \\\\ k & =-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, let ${t}_{1}$ be the time when the temperature of the tea is ${40}^{∘}C.$ Then, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}T({t}_{1}) & =40 \\\\ 28+32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =40 \\\\ 32{e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =12 \\\\ {e}^{-k{t}_{1}} & =\\frac{3}{8} \\\\ -k{t}_{1} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8}) \\\\ {t}_{1} & =-\\frac{1}{k}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting in our value for $k,$ we conclude that the tea will have a temperature of ${40}^{∘}C$ after\n\n$$\n{t}_{1}=\\frac{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{8})}{-\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{3}{4})}≈34\\,\\text{min},\n$$\n\nrounded to the nearest minute."} :step/id #uuid "a73cffdd-cb0c-5cc4-9384-d7e65e74f16d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186487419}}] :activity/title "Inhibited Decay Models With First-Order ODEs" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186486588}]}
